WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
Speaker 1: Republican leaders across the&nbsp;
country have been knocking Joe Biden for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:08.520
high inflation rate and the looming recession,&nbsp;
saying that if only we had a steady hand in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.780
White House, if only we had Republican leadership,&nbsp;
we wouldn't be in all these different problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:18.480
But unfortunately for them, these red states are&nbsp;
dealing with inflation, too. And in many of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.460
areas, within these red states, the inflation&nbsp;
rates are above the national average. Like, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.260
example, in Texas, we have Governor Greg Abbott&nbsp;
who's been attacking Joe Biden for inflation. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:35.040
if you look at the inflation rate in the Houston&nbsp;
area, it's 10.2% and it's 9.4% in the Dallas area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:40.980
which is quite a bit higher than the national&nbsp;
average of 8.5%. Florida Governor Ron DeSantis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.060
and Georgia Governor Brian Kemp have also&nbsp;
been knocking Biden for the inflation rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:51.420
even though in the Miami area inflation&nbsp;
is at 10.6%, in Tampa, 11.2%, and Atlanta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:57.480
11.5%. But at the same time, many of these same&nbsp;
Republicans who are attacking Biden for inflation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:03.000
they're taking credit for the strong jobs numbers&nbsp;
that are happening in their states. The Associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.100
Press found a familiar pattern in 15 Republican&nbsp;
led states in which governors on Twitter would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.120
praise job growth in their states. So like,&nbsp;
for example, we have Governor Greg Abbott from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:17.460
Texas. He was saying last month, quote, The U.S.&nbsp;
economy is in a recession under Biden. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.780
Texas was number one in the nation for&nbsp;
job growth in June. And more Texans have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:27.000
jobs today than ever before in our state's&nbsp;
history. Now, even though it's dishonest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.920
it actually does make sense for these Republican&nbsp;
governors to make the case from a political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.320
standpoint that the economy is so great in their&nbsp;
states, but in the toilet nationally. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.360
because voters tend to not be able to see through&nbsp;
it. They hold the state economy and the national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.040
economy to different standards. Let's say you're&nbsp;
in Florida and things are not going so well for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.720
you. You're going to be more likely to blame&nbsp;
Joe Biden for the inflation rate, gas prices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.500
what have you. Then you would be Governor Ron&nbsp;
DeSantis. You don't think that the census has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:00.960
much to do with it, but if you extend this logic&nbsp;
a little bit further, you see how absurd it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.800
right? Because you could look beyond the state,&nbsp;
you could look beyond the country and just look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.320
at things from the world level. And you would&nbsp;
understand that the entire world right now is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.520
dealing with inflation in some form. It's just one&nbsp;
of those things where we're trying to get back on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.500
track post the pandemic. And if you look at the&nbsp;
European Union, they have to raise interest rates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:26.220
now because their inflation rate is at a sky high&nbsp;
8.9%. Canada also has high inflation. They're at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:32.700
7.6%. This is not a issue that is unique to the&nbsp;
United States, but because so many Americans and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:38.220
Republicans especially seem to be unaware that&nbsp;
there is a world outside of the U.S., it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:43.680
just all about us. And all of our economies are&nbsp;
interconnected because they don't realize that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.060
They just think that the buck stops with Biden&nbsp;
entirely on this which which it doesn't. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.720
you know, it's not like you can just pass some&nbsp;
policy or decide to spend less money or whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:57.060
and poof, the inflation problem would be gone.&nbsp;
It's a lot more complicated that than that. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.740
you know, the truth is governors nor presidents&nbsp;
have much to do with a lot of these economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:06.360
indicators, like inflation, like gas prices.&nbsp;
And we'd be much better served if the general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:12.180
public knew that. But for now, with the public&nbsp;
being ignorant on it, Republicans are able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.820
take advantage of the public cigarets and keep&nbsp;
winning elections, unfortunately. We're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:22.740
to go to a quick break. We'll be back with much&nbsp;
more of The David Pakman Show right after this.

